tasks.content:
Sorting is one of the most common tasks in computing, and bubble sort is often the first sorting algorithm students learn because its idea is so visual: repeatedly step through a list, compare each pair of neighbouring elements, and swap them if they are in the wrong order. After one full pass through the list, the largest unsorted value has 'bubbled up' to its correct position at the end, which is where the algorithm gets its name.

Here is a working implementation:

def bubble_sort(numbers):
    n = len(numbers)
    for i in range(n - 1):
        for j in range(n - 1 - i):
            if numbers[j] > numbers[j + 1]:
                numbers[j], numbers[j + 1] = numbers[j + 1], numbers[j]
    return numbers

The outer loop controls how many passes are made, and the inner loop compares adjacent pairs, swapping them using Python's convenient tuple-swap syntax a, b = b, a. Notice that the inner loop's range shrinks each pass (n - 1 - i), because after each pass the largest remaining value is already correctly placed at the end, so there is no need to check it again.

Bubble sort is easy to understand and implement, but it is not efficient for large lists. In the worst case, where the list is in reverse order, it must make roughly n squared comparisons and swaps for a list of n items, which is written as O(n squared) time complexity. This means that doubling the size of the list roughly quadruples the work required, making bubble sort impractical for sorting large amounts of data compared to faster algorithms like merge sort, which you will meet later.

A useful improvement is to add a flag that tracks whether any swap happened during a pass; if a full pass completes with no swaps, the list is already sorted and the algorithm can stop early, which helps on nearly-sorted data. Despite its inefficiency on large datasets, bubble sort remains valuable for learning because it demonstrates core sorting concepts clearly: comparison, swapping, and repeated passes, ideas that reappear, in more sophisticated forms, in every other sorting algorithm you will study.

