Searching for a specific value inside a list is another fundamental problem in computing, and the approach you choose can make a huge difference to performance. The simplest method is linear search, which checks each element one at a time, from the start of the list, until it finds a match or reaches the end.

def linear_search(items, target):
    for index, value in enumerate(items):
        if value == target:
            return index
    return -1

Linear search works on any list, sorted or not, but in the worst case it has to check every single element, giving it a time complexity of O(n): if the list doubles in size, the worst-case search time roughly doubles too.

If your list is already sorted, you can use a much faster approach called binary search. Instead of checking every element, binary search repeatedly looks at the middle value of the remaining range and compares it to the target: if the middle value is too big, it discards the right half of the list; if it is too small, it discards the left half; and it repeats this on the smaller remaining half until it finds the value or runs out of elements to check.

def binary_search(items, target):
    low, high = 0, len(items) - 1
    while low <= high:
        mid = (low + high) // 2
        if items[mid] == target:
            return mid
        elif items[mid] < target:
            low = mid + 1
        else:
            high = mid - 1
    return -1

Because binary search eliminates half of the remaining possibilities with each comparison, its time complexity is O(log n), dramatically faster than linear search on large lists: searching a sorted list of one million items takes at most around twenty comparisons, compared to up to a million comparisons for linear search.

The critical requirement for binary search is that the list must already be sorted; running it on an unsorted list gives unreliable, incorrect results, because the algorithm relies on being able to rule out entire halves of the list based on the ordering. Choosing between linear and binary search is therefore a trade-off: linear search is simpler and works on any list, while binary search is far faster but only applicable once the data has been sorted, which itself takes time.

