tasks.content:
By now you have learned how to define classes, create objects, store attributes, write methods, and handle errors, so it is time to combine these skills into a complete, working program. Good object-oriented design starts with identifying the real-world thing you want to model, then deciding what data it needs to store as attributes and what actions it needs to perform as methods. Consider a simple bank account program:

class BankAccount:
    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):
        self.owner = owner
        self.balance = balance

    def deposit(self, amount):
        if amount <= 0:
            print('Deposit amount must be positive.')
            return
        self.balance += amount
        print(f'{self.owner} deposited {amount}. New balance: {self.balance}')

    def withdraw(self, amount):
        try:
            if amount > self.balance:
                raise ValueError('Insufficient funds')
            self.balance -= amount
            print(f'{self.owner} withdrew {amount}. New balance: {self.balance}')
        except ValueError as error:
            print(f'Withdrawal failed: {error}')

account = BankAccount('Priya', 100)
account.deposit(50)
account.withdraw(200)

This program brings together several ideas from earlier lessons: __init__ sets up the initial attributes owner and balance; deposit() and withdraw() are methods that change those attributes safely, validating input before making changes; and withdraw() even uses try and except together with Python's raise keyword, which lets you deliberately trigger an exception when a rule, such as not withdrawing more money than is available, has been broken.

Notice how the account's state, its balance, persists and updates correctly across multiple method calls on the same object, which is exactly the behaviour object-oriented programming is designed to support: real-world entities that have data and change over time in controlled, predictable ways.

When building your own class-based programs, a sensible process is to first sketch out, in plain English or pseudocode, what attributes the object needs and what actions it must support; then write __init__ to set up those attributes; then write one method at a time, testing each with simple example objects before moving to the next; and finally add validation and error handling once the basic behaviour works correctly. This step-by-step approach, building and testing incrementally rather than writing an entire class at once, will help you avoid and quickly find bugs as your programs grow more ambitious.

