tasks.content:
Throughout this course you have met several algorithms, and comparing them fairly requires a standard way to describe how their running time grows as the amount of data increases. This is exactly what Big O notation provides: a way of describing an algorithm's efficiency in terms of how the number of operations grows relative to the size of the input, n, generally focusing on the worst case, and ignoring constant factors and smaller details that matter less as n gets large.

The most common complexities you will encounter, from fastest to slowest, are O(1), constant time, where the number of operations does not depend on the size of the input at all, such as accessing a single element of a list by its index, my_list[0]; O(log n), logarithmic time, such as binary search, where each step eliminates roughly half of the remaining data; O(n), linear time, such as linear search or a single loop through a list, where the work grows directly in proportion to the input size; O(n log n), such as merge sort, which splits the data logarithmically but still does linear work at each level; and O(n squared), quadratic time, such as bubble sort, where nested loops mean the work grows much faster than the input size.

def get_first(items):
    return items[0]

def has_duplicate(items):
    for i in range(len(items)):
        for j in range(len(items)):
            if i != j and items[i] == items[j]:
                return True
    return False

get_first runs in O(1) time, since it always performs exactly one operation regardless of how long items is. has_duplicate, however, uses nested loops that each run roughly n times, giving O(n squared) time, since for every element it checks against every other element.

Big O notation deliberately ignores constant multipliers and smaller terms, because what matters most for large inputs is the dominant growth pattern: an algorithm that does 3n operations and one that does n operations are both still considered O(n), since doubling n roughly doubles the work in both cases, whereas an O(n squared) algorithm's work grows far faster, quadrupling when n doubles. Understanding Big O lets you predict, without running any code, whether an algorithm will remain practical as your data grows from a handful of items to millions, which is essential knowledge for writing software that performs well in the real world.

