tasks.content:
A capstone project brings together everything you have learned this year into a single, complete piece of software, and succeeding at one requires more planning than any single earlier exercise, because you are now responsible for designing the overall structure yourself rather than following a template. The most important first step is decomposition: breaking the overall project down into smaller, manageable parts before writing any code, then deciding how those parts, often represented as classes and functions, will work together.

Consider a simple library management capstone. You might decompose it into a Book class to represent each book as an object, a function to save the current collection of books to a file so data is not lost between runs, a function to load that data back in when the program starts, and a sorting function to arrange books by title or by year.

class Book:
    def __init__(self, title, author, year):
        self.title = title
        self.author = author
        self.year = year

    def __str__(self):
        return f'{self.title} by {self.author} ({self.year})'

def save_books(books, filename):
    with open(filename, 'w') as file:
        for book in books:
            file.write(f'{book.title},{book.author},{book.year}\n')

This snippet shows a Book class with a special __str__ method, which controls how an object is displayed when printed, and a save_books function that writes each book's data to a file as a comma-separated line, combining object-oriented design directly with file handling, two skills you learned separately earlier in the course.

Good capstone practice means planning the structure on paper first, sketching what classes and functions you need and how data flows between them, then building and testing incrementally: get file saving and loading working correctly with one book before adding sorting, and get sorting working correctly before adding a search feature, rather than writing the entire program at once and only testing it at the very end, when a bug could be hiding almost anywhere.

Throughout the project, apply what you learned about error handling, so invalid input does not crash the program; about readable code, so your classes and functions have clear, well-documented names; and about testing, writing simple checks for each piece as you build it. A well-planned capstone project demonstrates not just that you can write individual pieces of code, but that you can design, combine, and test a complete, working system, which is exactly the kind of skill a real software project demands.

As you prepare for your GCSE-style coding exam, it is worth reviewing how this year's topics build a coherent, connected picture of more advanced programming. You began with list comprehensions and advanced string methods, learning to write compact expressions like [x for x in range(10) if x % 2 == 0] and to manipulate text with split(), join(), and f-strings. You then studied recursion, understanding that every working recursive function needs a base case and a recursive case, before applying that idea directly to merge sort, a divide-and-conquer algorithm with O(n log n) time complexity, far more efficient on large lists than bubble sort's O(n squared).

You were introduced to binary search trees conceptually, seeing how a branching, ordered structure of nodes allows searching in roughly O(log n) time when balanced, and you studied Big O notation formally, learning to classify algorithms by how their running time grows: O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), and O(n squared), from fastest to slowest as input size grows large. You then moved into professional practices: testing with assert statements and edge cases, debugging systematically rather than by guesswork, understanding Git concepts like commits, branches, and merge conflicts, and writing readable, well-documented code using PEP 8 conventions, meaningful names, and docstrings, all of which you finally combined in a capstone project.

For exam success, practise tracing algorithms by hand, one line at a time, exactly as the computer would execute them:

def mystery(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return n
    return mystery(n - 1) + mystery(n - 2)
print(mystery(4))

Tracing this carefully, mystery(4) calls mystery(3) + mystery(2); mystery(3) calls mystery(2) + mystery(1); mystery(2) calls mystery(1) + mystery(0); working all the way down and back up gives mystery(4) a final value of 3, and recognising this pattern of calls, this is the recursive Fibonacci sequence, is a common exam task.

In the exam, identify precisely what a question is asking, whether it wants an output trace, an explanation of a bug, a Big O classification, or a specific technical term, and answer in exactly that form; for code-tracing questions, write out intermediate variable values as you go rather than trying to compute the answer in your head, since partial working often earns credit even if your final answer has a small slip. Revising by writing and testing your own short programs for each topic, not just reading through notes, is the most reliable way to consolidate a full year of increasingly advanced coding skills.
